<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title><%= content_for(:title) || "McRitchie Studio" %></title>
    <%= csrf_meta_tags %>
    <%= render "layouts/studio/head" %>
  </head>

  <body x-data class="bg-page text-body min-h-screen" :class="{ 'dev-mode': $store.devMode }">
    <%= render "layouts/studio/flash" %>
    <%# Link-sidebar store bridge: engine-owned since studio-engine 0.30 —
        components/_link_sidebar carries it. %>
    <%# The bar stack is the header's SIBLING, not its child (studio-engine 0.33):
        bars compose independently of the navbar. Since 0.39 the stack sits in
        NORMAL FLOW and reserves its own height, so the header pins at a static
        top-0 and a taller bar — or a second one, like impersonation — pushes the
        header down with no change here. Nothing measures, so there is no
        published height to offset by. %>
    <%= render "studio/banners/stack" %>
    <%# The header is the engine's navbar (layouts/_navbar): its nav-collapse
        Stimulus controller writes --nav-p on the header from scrollY once per
        animation frame, and data-pin publishes --pin-nav-h / --pin-nav-bottom,
        which the app strip and the board's lane headers position off (see
        _app_ladder_row and _deploy_board). It renders the link sidebar too,
        because Studio.sidebar_sections is set. ShellHelper#hub_navbar adds the
        heading rule: the brand is the h1 only on a page with none of its own. %>
    <%= hub_navbar(page_html: yield) %>

    <% if content_for?(:full_width) %>
      <%= yield %>
    <% else %>
      <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 py-6">
        <%= yield %>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# The site footer and its booking popup (studio-engine; the facts and the
        pages it shows on are in config/initializers/studio.rb). %>
    <%= studio_site_footer %>

    <%# Modal host (engine) + the app's modal partials. Powers sign-in (auth),
        the "check your inbox" magic-link confirmation and the first-name
        onboarding step. %>
    <%# Per-modal card widths, BY MODAL ID (studio-engine's registry): read by
        the host when it renders, so this must come BEFORE the host. The sign-in
        card needs more than the default max-w-sm for its Google button and
        two-line title. %>
    <script>
      window.StudioModals = window.StudioModals || {};
      window.StudioModals.CARD_WIDTHS = Object.assign({}, window.StudioModals.CARD_WIDTHS || {}, { 'auth': 'max-w-md' });
    </script>
    <%= render "studio/modals/host" do %>
      <template x-if="$store.modals.current().id === 'auth'">
        <%= render "modals/auth" %>
      </template>
      <template x-if="$store.modals.current().id === 'check-inbox'">
        <%= render "modals/check_inbox" %>
      </template>
      <%# The shared first-name step (engine primitive). Rendered from the gem so
          this app and turf-monster ask the same question the same way; the
          endpoints behind it come from config.draw_onboarding_routes. %>
      <template x-if="$store.modals.current().id === 'onboarding-first-name'">
        <%= render "studio/modals/onboarding/first_name",
                   subtext: "Just your first name — we use it to address you in emails." %>
      </template>
    <% end %>

    <%# Ask a freshly signed-in account for a first name, once. The SERVER decides
        whether it is still outstanding (blank field, not skipped this session);
        the client only opens what it was told to.

        THE MARKER IS THE SIGNAL, NOT THE SCRIPT — and that distinction is the
        whole fix for a real bug. Handlers live on `document`, which SURVIVES a
        Turbo Drive body replacement; the emitted <script> does not. So an
        earlier page's handler keeps firing on later pages whose server render
        deliberately contains no arming block, and it re-asked a user who had
        just answered — on every navigation, for the life of the tab. Guarding on
        the modal stack could not stop it: close() splices the entry off `stack`
        after the exit animation, so by the time the stale handler ran the stack
        was empty and the guard waved it through.

        So the handler reads the CURRENT document at fire time. The marker below
        is rendered only while the ask is outstanding, and Turbo replaces the body
        with it absent — a stale handler looks, finds nothing, and does nothing.

        Armed on two events because either can be the one that happens: a full
        load fires alpine:initialized, while a Turbo visit does not (Alpine is
        already up), so turbo:load is the only signal there. Attached ONCE per
        document — without the flag, every visit added another pair and they
        accumulated for the life of the tab. %>
    <% if logged_in? && Studio.first_name_outstanding?(current_user, session) %>
      <div id="onboarding-ask-first-name" hidden></div>
    <% end %>
    <script>
      (function () {
        if (window.__onboardingAskArmed) return;
        window.__onboardingAskArmed = true;

        function askFirstName() {
          // Read the page we are on NOW, not the one this script shipped with.
          if (!document.getElementById('onboarding-ask-first-name')) return;
          if (!window.Alpine || !Alpine.store) return;
          var store = Alpine.store('modals');
          if (!store || typeof store.open !== 'function') return;
          var already = (store.stack || []).some(function (entry) {
            return entry && entry.id === 'onboarding-first-name';
          });
          if (already) return;
          // dismissible: false — escape and click-outside would close this
          // WITHOUT posting the skip, which silently abandons the ask the same
          // way the partial's own × exists to prevent. Save and Skip both post;
          // they are the only two ways out, and both settle the question.
          store.open('onboarding-first-name', { dismissible: false });
        }

        // THE MARKER MUST NOT OUTLIVE THE ASK.
        //
        // Reading the current document fixed FORWARD navigation, because the
        // server stops emitting the marker on the next render. It does NOT fix a
        // Turbo RESTORATION visit: the back button replays a CACHED body, and the
        // snapshot was taken while the marker was still sitting in the DOM — so
        // the handler found it and re-asked someone who had already answered,
        // with dismissible:false leaving them no way out but answering twice.
        //
        // The server cannot help here (a cached restore issues no request), so
        // the client retires the marker the moment the ask is settled. The engine
        // partial already announces that on BOTH paths — it dispatches
        // onboarding-step-done after a successful save AND after a skip — so this
        // listens rather than duplicating the rule.
        window.addEventListener('onboarding-step-done', function () {
          var marker = document.getElementById('onboarding-ask-first-name');
          if (marker) marker.remove();
        });

        // And never let this modal be SNAPSHOTTED OPEN. dismissible:false is what
        // stops escape/click-outside from abandoning the ask, but it also exempts
        // this modal from the host's closeAllDismissible sweep on
        // turbo:before-cache — so a back button pressed while it is open would
        // restore a body with the modal already mounted, on top of the handler
        // mounting it again. Close it ourselves before the snapshot is taken.
        document.addEventListener('turbo:before-cache', function () {
          var store = window.Alpine && Alpine.store && Alpine.store('modals');
          if (!store || typeof store.close !== 'function') return;
          var mounted = (store.stack || []).some(function (entry) {
            return entry && entry.id === 'onboarding-first-name';
          });
          if (mounted) store.close();
        });

        document.addEventListener('alpine:initialized', askFirstName);
        document.addEventListener('turbo:load', askFirstName);
      })();
    </script>

    <%# Magic-link request. Inline so it is always defined before the auth
        card's Alpine form or the check-inbox modal can call it. %>
    <script>
      // `returnTo` (optional) is where the emailed link lands after sign-in —
      // /build passes its draft's URL so the prompt survives the round trip,
      // even when the email is opened on another device. The server keeps it
      // to a same-origin path.
      window.postMagicLink = async function (email, returnTo) {
        var body = new URLSearchParams();
        body.set('email', email || '');
        if (returnTo) body.set('return_to', returnTo);
        var resp = await fetch('/magic_link', {
          method: 'POST',
          headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded', 'Accept': 'application/json',
                     'X-CSRF-Token': document.querySelector('meta[name="csrf-token"]')?.content || '' },
          body: body.toString()
        });
        var payload = {};
        try { payload = await resp.json(); } catch (e) { payload = {}; }
        if (!resp.ok || payload.success === false) throw new Error(payload.error || 'Magic link request failed');
        return Object.assign({ success: true }, payload);
      };
    </script>

    <%# window.studioBoard — the Alpine factory behind the studio/board primitive
        (the tasks, news, and content kanban boards; the depth chart is still
        bespoke, pending primitive support for its within-lane depth/lane shape).
        It MUST live at page level: a <script> inside the board component template
        would be cloned by Alpine and never run. Vendored SortableJS is already
        loaded globally by layouts/studio/_head. %>
    <%= render "studio/board_assets" %>

    <%# The "at" format's re-stamper, owned by studio-engine (Studio::AtTimeHelper).
        The hub's local fork is retired: the two copies were kept byte-identical BY
        HAND, and the Indiana/Louisville zone-alias bug had to be fixed twice. One
        copy, in the gem, is the whole point of this render.

        Installed page-level for the whole app, not per-view: any page may render an
        at_time_tag, and every one of them needs the viewer's clock and flag.
        Self-guarded, so a second render is a no-op.

        LAST in the body, deliberately. Its first pass runs synchronously as the
        script parses, so moving it into head finds zero stamps on that pass and
        leaves every one of them showing the app-timezone fallback until the next
        sweep. Guarded by test/integration/at_time_gem_adoption_test.rb. %>
    <%= render "studio/at_time_script" %>
  </body>
</html>
